00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:52.880
And I was living in Ridgewood, Queens, and I was commuting to Hell&#x27;s Kitchen for work. My friend got me a production job at this crazy content studio. And that was my first summer in the station, in New York. And I was really fascinated by how hot it was getting in the subway stations. And one day I just had an idea to get one of these digital thermometer guns and start pointing at the surfaces in 14th Street, New York Square, which was my transfer station. And film a TikTok, which makes me sometimes.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:21:19.490
I want to puke to even say that. But I made a couple videos in 2024 and they gained a lot of traction among people living in New York. And everyone was like, you know, go to my station, 34th Street, Herald Square, go to 181st One Station, Elevator, it&#x27;s the hottest place we&#x27;ve ever been. I realized that people have a very visceral connection with their station. We all have our station that we go through. And it made me think that we have...

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:48.460
This is an issue that everyone knows about. Millions of people every day are experiencing it. But it seems underreported on. And also, the solutions seem doing great. Everyone&#x27;s saying that, you know, MTA is just, where&#x27;s the money going? And it seems very kind of pessimistic. So, there&#x27;s some of the comments that I would get online around, like, yeah, MTA getting billions in eight packages every year. Money always goes poof. Or, you know, maybe figure out how to ventilate. Or, you know, look out for the elderly and young children.

00:21:48.880 --> 00:22:18.000
Also around, I thought this was really interesting, like, being on SSRIs during the summers, especially Dangerous and Miserable. Or, how some of that they were going to pass out, who&#x27;s so on Canal Street. So, that was the summer of 2024, I just made a couple of those videos. But it really got me thinking, you know, I&#x27;ve done these projects before, like, in public space, and I&#x27;m just generally curious about how people move through the city. So, it&#x27;s this thing where, like...

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:46.930
It&#x27;s hot now. We had a heatwave last week. But then it gets cold again in the fall and we sort of forget about it. But it&#x27;s ultimately going to come back again even hotter every summer. And it seems like we kind of just forget about it. And it comes back and we&#x27;re like, what are we going to do? And everyone forgets about it. Whereas I find it really interesting with other natural disasters like flooding or hurricanes or other things that leaves visible residue. With the flooding, we can see the grates. There&#x27;s leaves over it. We can see the flooded streets. There&#x27;s water in the stations.

00:22:46.930 --> 00:23:14.700
So we are constantly reminded of it. With the heat in the summer, we forget about it. So I was thinking about how to take advantage of this issue that everyone experiences. I put the dates on here. November of 2024. I remember I texted my friend Will. I went back into computer science and I was curious if it was like...

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:45.680
Possible to install sensors in stations and start to actually track heat and humidity data because the MTA doesn&#x27;t actually, well, I&#x27;ve learned more now, but it doesn&#x27;t seem like they actually have any data around how hot it is or what they&#x27;re trying to do about it. So it&#x27;s like, okay, well, if they&#x27;re not doing it, is there anything we can do? And also in New York, it seems like you can do whatever you want in terms of art. You can put stuff in stations and no one&#x27;s going to say anything or you can put a placard on a broken display. No one really cares. So it&#x27;s kind of right for...

00:23:45.710 --> 00:24:12.320
Artistic experimentation. And around the same time as that, when I was asking my friend Will if I could hack into a thermometer and somehow start to stream data. This was in February of 2025. So it says that New York City&#x27;s subway is using Google Pixels to listen for trap defects. And I thought this was really interesting. So New York City and the MTA partnered with Google Public Sector. So Google Public Sector is...

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:40.300
A branch of Google that uses their private funds to push public projects. And I guess currently what happens, there&#x27;s specific engineers at the MTA who ride through the trains and listen for defects. I guess you can tell by what it sounds like. And through just listening, they can tell what parts of the tracks need repaired. And what Google did is they just had a bunch of cheap phones with a lav mic within the train and started collecting as much audio data as possible.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:25:06.990
And then train some type of model where they could programmatically tell what parts of the system needed fixed. So that was an example of using relatively inexpensive technology and partnering with the city to solve a problem that ultimately before was someone just going around and doing it themselves.

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:37.900
Data collection device. This is in February of 2025. Here, this is a Xiaomi thermometer for like $3 that I got off Alibaba or something. This is an Arduino port, which is like a microcomputer that you can, you know, put simple like C++ code into. And this is a portable battery. And really, you&#x27;re able to crack these thermometers and put certain code in them that will make it stream its data to the computer. And then you can make the computer like...

00:25:37.900 --> 00:26:07.380
Stream the temperature data to, you know, a database and start to collect long-term data. It looked like a bomb. And basically for, like, again, I made those videos in 2024, the summer of 2024. From then until, like, the next one, basically, it&#x27;s, like, downtime. So I was thinking, I was, like, in the station so much, doing research with this thing. This is, like, a photo someone took of me. I was, like, in 14 Street Bean Square a lot, like, in 2025.

