I was getting all this shit over my hands, but I was putting that thing in the rafters of the station, trying to figure out where it could be seen, but also where it could still connect to internet. There&#x27;s a SIM card attached to this as well, so it doesn&#x27;t connect to Wi-Fi, it actually is using data to stream the data. And it didn&#x27;t really work.

00:26:35.470 --> 00:27:04.300
There&#x27;s a lot of Bluetooth interference in the train stations with all the metal poles and all the different devices down there that we don&#x27;t really see. The advertising digital screens use a bunch of technology too. It&#x27;s the point where I would test it out in my room and it would stream the data perfectly. But then when I would put it out in the train station, it wouldn&#x27;t work at all. So I was carrying my laptop into 14 Street Community Square every week, trying to debug it there. And it just...

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:33.380
Was really cumbersome and started to not be fun. So what I ended up doing, I found these Kestrel drop, like basically temperature loggers that would store the data on board. So it just seemed easier. It was kind of a built-in solution. I wouldn&#x27;t have to actually develop it myself. So I bought like 10 of these and I put tape over them. So this is in June now of 2025. That previous picture was in February. Put tape over them.

00:27:33.380 --> 00:28:00.590
And I put magnets on the back of them because all those metal rafters in the stations are metal so they&#x27;re obviously magnetic. And what I did then was I hid them within the rafters of like 10 or so high traffic stations in Manhattan. And it was actually a map. So I put one in Broadway Lafayette. I put two in 14th Sydney Square. I put one in 34th Street Road Square.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:26.750
Put one in Hudson Yards. Put one in Times Square. I put one in 168 One Station and then I put one in the elevator in the 181st One Station. Like behind the security camera. The MTA puts out kind of foot traffic reports. So some of these were largely based on what are the highest foot traffic stations in the system and then some like these two up here in Washington Heights were more

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:56.940
Anecdotal data of people telling me online, like, you gotta visit these stations. And, you know, what I&#x27;m thinking about, this whole time I&#x27;ve been thinking about it is, why are we doing this? And like, when I was putting it here, I was trying to be sort of intentional about how I was putting them or where I was putting them. The goal is to try to get the best representation of what it feels like for a commuter to stand next to the subway platform waiting for the train. So I was like, can I get it like three to eight feet off the ground?

00:28:56.940 --> 00:29:25.890
Facing the platform. So I&#x27;m sure as you all know, when the train pulls on the platform in the summer, the exhaust from the ACs blows onto your face. That&#x27;s a large reason why it&#x27;s so hot in the stations in the summer. That and the friction from the brakes. So I was trying to be very intentional at where I was placing them. This is a lot of the quote-unquote research that I was doing last summer. And this is what the data looked like. So I was recording...

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:55.570
A data point every five minutes at all these stations, and it was recording temperature, humidity, and heat index. And heat index is the feels like temperature. It&#x27;s like some type of calculation of heat and humidity, right? Because it can be 90 degrees out, but it&#x27;s also, you know, subtropical environment in New York and in the station, so it&#x27;s not actually representing what it feels like. So I wanted to get that, like, it was all about what does it feel like for a commuter actually to be in the station. And again, to go back to those comments that I...

